      "latency_ms": 2332.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 581,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (the water we drink, rivers, oceans)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity in the air)\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater has several fascinating and unusual properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it carries essential nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through our bodies and through ecosystems.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps body temperatures stable.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water, preventing lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, thus protecting aquatic life.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View\n* **Earth's Lifeline:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered by water, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Water is constantly recycled through evaporation (turning into vapor from heat), condensation (forming clouds), precipitation (rain, snow), and collection. The water you drink today has been recycled by the Earth for billions of years.\n* **Fuel for Life:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. It lubricates joints, regulates body temperature, flushes out waste, and acts as the medium for almost all cellular chemical reactions. Without water, living organisms quickly dehydrate and die.\n\nIn short, water is the quiet, powerful force that shapes our planet’s geography, drives its weather, and makes the existence of biological life possible."
